/*!
    \title Qt for HarmonyOS Notification Example
    \example platform/harmonynotification
    \examplecategory {Mobile}
    \meta tag {widgets,harmonyos,notification}
    \ingroup corelib_examples

    \brief Posts a notification from a Qt application on HarmonyOS.

    The example asks the user to allow notifications for the application. It
    then posts a notification with the title and text entered in its window.
    It uses the platform's \c {@ohos.notificationManager} module.

    The button handlers hand the work to the JS thread. The title and text
    are collected on the Qt thread as a QJsonObject:

    \snippet platform/harmonynotification/main.cpp 0

    \c requestEnableNotification() shows the platform dialog in which the
    user allows notifications. It takes the \c UIAbilityContext of the
    default ability, and the dialog is bound to that context:

    \snippet platform/harmonynotification/main.cpp 1

    \c publish() takes a notification request, which the task builds on the
    JS thread. The content type comes from the \c ContentType enumeration of
    the module. The title and text are mapped from the QJsonObject with
    QtHarmony::mapQJsonObjectToJsObject():

    \snippet platform/harmonynotification/main.cpp 2

    Both calls return a promise. The handlers attached to the promise report
    the outcome in the window. They post the text to the Qt thread with
    QNativeInterface::QHarmonyApplication::runOnQtThread(). They pass a
    QPointer to the status label, which was created on the Qt thread. The
    posted functor checks the QPointer before it updates the label:

    \snippet platform/harmonynotification/main.cpp 3

    The window also shows an error that a call throws before its promise
    exists, and a missing module or ability.

    \section1 Running the Example

    Press \uicontrol {Allow notifications} and allow notifications in the
    dialog. Then press \uicontrol {Post notification}. While notifications
    are not allowed, \c publish() fails with a "Notification disabled"
    error, which the window shows. The request uses the default notification
    ID, so posting again updates the notification instead of adding another
    one.
*/
